\pystr sets how text printed by Python is typed into a
pane. Python code can print text that starts with
"livelily: " (or "livelily-2: " for pane 2), and the rest
of the text is typed into the pane as if it was written
with the keyboard.

\pystr takes one argument, char or line:

\pystr char

types the text one character at a time, one character
per frame. This is the default.

\pystr line

inserts the text one whole line at a time.

The setting is global, not per pane. Any other argument
gives an "unknown argument" error.
